=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-intentions-146] Implementing Performance-Gated Independent AI Auditing: Argument from Analogy — The FDA pharmaceutical analogy is structurally strained: drugs have discrete chemical identities and dose-response curves amenable to clinical trials, whereas AI models are dynamic, context-sensitive systems whose 'failure modes' emerge from open-ended interactions—making pre-deployment gating far less predictive of real-world harm.
- [skp-intentions-055] Operationalize AI Risk Mitigation Through Concrete Technical Practices: Techno-solutionism / Reductive fallacy — The node frames AI risk mitigation primarily as a technical and design problem, potentially underweighting governance failures, perverse incentives, and sociotechnical dynamics that no amount of good engineering can resolve.
- [skp-intentions-110] Establishing Decentralized User-Controlled Cryptographic Receipts for Algorithmic Discovery: Availability Heuristic / Techno-Legal Solutionism — The proposal treats cryptographic logging and statutory discovery as sufficient accountability mechanisms, potentially overstating the practical value of telemetry access when the underlying interpretability problem remains unsolved.
- [skp-beliefs-164] Companies Use Narrow Audits to Deflect Meaningful AI Oversight: Motive attribution fallacy — The claim presupposes intentional deception or deflection as the dominant corporate motive, conflating the observable outcome (narrow audit scope) with a specific bad-faith intent, when resource constraints, legal liability concerns, or immature standards could produce identical behavior.
- [skp-beliefs-167] Alarmist, Optimistic, and Political Framings of AI Serve Distinct Agendas: Genetic fallacy — The node implies that identifying the agenda behind a narrative is sufficient to undermine its validity — but the origin or motivation of a claim does not determine its truth. Alarmist rhetoric about AI risks could still be correct even if it serves particular interests.
- [skp-beliefs-161] Audit Taxonomies Are Thinking Aids, Not Rigid Compliance Checklists: False Dichotomy — The node sets up a binary between 'rigid compliance checklists' and 'flexible thinking aids,' obscuring the possibility that structured, standardized frameworks can coexist with and complement adaptive human judgment.
- [skp-beliefs-129] Safety Rhetoric as Instrument for Regulatory Consolidation: Genetic fallacy — The argument risks dismissing existential risk concerns entirely on the basis of who funds or promotes them, rather than engaging with the arguments on their merits. Even if some proponents benefit commercially, that does not establish the falsity of their safety claims.
- [skp-beliefs-095] Large Language Models Create Systemic Risks to Data Integrity, Privacy, and Epistemic Quality: Composition fallacy — The node aggregates several distinct documented harms (data contamination, memorization-based privacy leakage, consent gaps) and treats their combination as a single, unified 'systemic' risk — but the systemic nature of the aggregate is not established just because each component harm is real.

